/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/ccsp.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("ccsp", [["cp1",0,1,"A department says its new 'private cloud' requires a ticket and two weeks of manual work by the infrastructure team for every new virtual machine. Which NIST essential characteristic is MOST clearly missing?",["Broad network access","Resource pooling","Measured service","On-demand self-service"],3,"On-demand self-service means consumers provision resources themselves without human interaction with the provider. A manual ticket process fails it; the other characteristics may still be present.","Domain 1",["Broad network access is about reaching services over standard networks from many devices, which the scenario does not contradict.","Resource pooling concerns serving many consumers from shared resources; a slow approval process does not show pooling is absent.","Measured service is metering and reporting usage, which can exist even when provisioning is manual.",null],2],["cp2",0,1,"In which cloud service model is the customer responsible for patching the guest operating system?",["Platform as a Service (PaaS)","Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS)","Software as a Service (SaaS)","Function as a Service (serverless)"],1,"In IaaS the customer manages everything from the guest operating system up. In PaaS, SaaS and serverless the provider manages the operating system.","Domain 1",["In PaaS the provider runs and patches the operating system and runtime; the customer manages its code and data.",null,"In SaaS the provider operates the whole application stack, including the operating system.","Serverless functions run on provider-managed infrastructure, so the customer never patches an operating system."],1],["cp3",0,1,"An employee shares a SaaS folder containing customer records with 'anyone with the link', and the records leak. Under the shared responsibility model, who is responsible for this failure?",["The customer, because sharing settings and data access are its responsibility","The SaaS provider, because it operates the application","The SaaS provider and customer equally, because SaaS responsibilities are shared evenly","The identity provider, because it authenticated the employee"],0,"In every service model the customer controls its data, users and configuration choices such as sharing. The provider's platform worked as designed.","Domain 1",[null,"Operating the application does not make the provider responsible for how the customer configures sharing of its own data.","SaaS responsibilities are not split evenly; data, access and configuration decisions sit with the customer.","The identity provider authenticated a legitimate user correctly; the leak came from a sharing decision, not an authentication failure."],1],["cp4",0,1,"Several regional hospitals with identical regulatory obligations want to share the cost of a cloud environment that only they can use. Which deployment model fits BEST?",["Public cloud","Hybrid cloud","Community cloud","Multi-cloud"],2,"A community cloud serves a group of organizations with shared concerns, such as the same regulations, and spreads governance and cost among them.","Domain 1",["A public cloud is open to any customer, which does not meet the goal of restricting use to the hospitals.","Hybrid cloud describes linking distinct clouds such as private and public, not sharing one environment among peers.",null,"Multi-cloud means using more than one provider; it says nothing about restricting use to a group of organizations."],1],["cp5",0,1,"Which party acts on a customer's behalf to negotiate, aggregate or integrate services from several cloud providers?",["Cloud service broker","Cloud auditor","Cloud service provider","Regulator"],0,"A cloud service broker is a partner that intermediates between customers and providers, negotiating and combining services.","Domain 1",[null,"A cloud auditor independently assesses controls; it does not negotiate or combine services for the customer.","The provider offers its own services; it does not act for the customer across other providers.","A regulator sets and enforces rules; it does not procure or integrate services."],1],["cp6",0,1,"A company is leaving a public cloud storage service and cannot supervise how the provider wipes its disks. What is the MOST reliable way to make its remaining data unrecoverable?",["Ask the provider to degauss the drives that held the data","Destroy the customer-controlled keys that encrypted the data","Overwrite each object once with zeros before deleting the account","Delete the objects and rely on the provider's standard deletion"],1,"Crypto-shredding works in shared infrastructure: if data was encrypted with keys only the customer controls, destroying all copies of the keys leaves every replica unreadable.","Domain 1",["Providers do not degauss specific drives for one tenant in shared storage, and degaussing does not work on SSDs.",null,"Overwriting through the service API cannot be verified across replicas and underlying media the customer never sees.","Standard deletion may leave recoverable remnants and replicas, so it gives the least assurance."],2],["cp7",0,1,"Why do containers generally provide weaker isolation than virtual machines?",["Containers cannot use network segmentation","Containers always run with root privileges on the host","Containers share the host operating system kernel","Containers store data only in ephemeral volumes"],2,"All containers on a host share its kernel, so a kernel vulnerability or privileged container can affect other containers; VMs each run their own kernel on a hypervisor.","Domain 1",["Containers can be segmented with network policies, so this is not the reason for weaker isolation.","Running as root is a common misconfiguration, not a requirement; containers can and should run as non-root users.",null,"Containers can use persistent volumes; storage type is unrelated to kernel-level isolation."],1],["cp8",0,1,"A partner bank will share card data for fraud analysis only if the cloud provider's administrators cannot read it even while it is being processed. Which technology addresses this requirement?",["Server-side encryption of all storage volumes and database snapshots","TLS between the partner bank and the application","Tokenization of the card data at rest","Confidential computing with a trusted execution environment"],3,"Confidential computing protects data in use by processing it inside a hardware enclave that the host OS and hypervisor cannot read.","Domain 1",["Storage encryption protects data at rest; the data is decrypted in memory during processing.","TLS protects data in transit only, not while the application processes it.","Tokenization replaces values at rest, but analysis of real card data would still expose it in memory.",null],2],["cp9",0,1,"A business impact analysis states that the order system may lose no more than 15 minutes of data. Which metric has been defined?",["Recovery time objective (RTO)","Recovery point objective (RPO)","Maximum tolerable downtime (MTD)","Mean time to repair (MTTR)"],1,"RPO is the maximum acceptable data loss measured in time before the disruption, which drives backup and replication frequency.","Domain 1",["RTO is how quickly the service must be restored, not how much data may be lost.",null,"MTD is the longest the business can tolerate an outage, which concerns downtime rather than data loss.","MTTR is an operational average for repairing components, not a business recovery target."],1],["cp10",0,1,"A customer wants the strongest independent evidence that a provider's security controls actually worked throughout the past year. Which report should it request?",["SOC 2 Type II","SOC 2 Type I","SOC 3","CSA STAR Level 1 self-assessment"],0,"A SOC 2 Type II report tests the operating effectiveness of controls over a period, typically six to twelve months.","Domain 1",[null,"A Type I report evaluates control design at a single point in time, not operation over a year.","SOC 3 is a general-use summary without the detailed test results needed for due diligence.","A STAR Level 1 entry is a self-assessment, so it is not independent evidence."],2],["cp11",0,1,"Which standard provides a code of practice specifically for protecting personally identifiable information in public clouds acting as processors?",["ISO/IEC 27017","ISO/IEC 27001","ISO/IEC 27018","ISO/IEC 15408"],2,"ISO/IEC 27018 focuses on PII protection by public cloud processors. ISO/IEC 27017 covers cloud security controls more generally.","Domain 1",["ISO/IEC 27017 gives cloud-specific security controls but is not focused on PII protection.","ISO/IEC 27001 specifies requirements for an ISMS, not cloud PII practices.",null,"ISO/IEC 15408 is Common Criteria, used to evaluate products."],2],["cp12",0,1,"A regulation requires that encryption protecting certain government data use cryptographic modules validated to the current U.S. standard. Which validation should the security team look for?",["Common Criteria EAL4","ISO/IEC 27017 certification","CSA STAR attestation","FIPS 140-3"],3,"FIPS 140-3 is the current U.S. standard for validating cryptographic modules, replacing FIPS 140-2.","Domain 1",["Common Criteria evaluates product assurance against a protection profile; it is not the cryptographic module validation named here.","ISO/IEC 27017 certifies cloud security controls in a management system, not individual cryptographic modules.","CSA STAR attestation covers a provider's controls against the CCM, not module validation.",null],2],["cp13",0,1,"Before approving a hosted large language model service for summarizing confidential contracts, what should the security team verify FIRST in the provider's terms?",["Whether the model scores well on public accuracy and reasoning benchmarks for legal text","Whether prompts and data are retained or used to train the provider's models","Whether the service supports the most languages","Whether the provider offers the lowest price per request"],1,"Confidential data sent in prompts may be stored or used for training; data use, retention and location terms are the key security and legal questions.","Domain 1",["Benchmark accuracy is a quality question and says nothing about how confidential data is handled.",null,"Language support is a functional requirement, not a data protection control.","Price is a procurement factor and does not address the confidentiality risk."],2],["cp14",0,1,"An attacker sends a support chatbot a message containing hidden instructions that make it reveal its system prompt and internal tool list. What type of attack is this?",["Data poisoning","Model inversion","Prompt injection","Membership inference"],2,"Prompt injection uses crafted input to override an AI model's instructions at runtime.","Domain 1",["Data poisoning corrupts training data before or during training, not a live conversation.","Model inversion tries to reconstruct training data from model outputs, not to override instructions.",null,"Membership inference tests whether a specific record was in the training set."],1],["cp15",0,1,"What is the main security drawback of adopting a multi-cloud strategy?",["More complexity in keeping identity, logging and policy consistent","Greater dependence on a single provider's proprietary services","Loss of the ability to encrypt data at rest","Inability to use infrastructure as code"],0,"Each provider has different identity, logging and security tooling, so consistent governance and skills are harder. Multi-cloud reduces, rather than increases, single-provider lock-in.","Domain 1",[null,"Multi-cloud reduces dependence on one provider; greater lock-in is the opposite of its effect.","All major providers support encryption at rest, so multi-cloud does not remove it.","Infrastructure as code tools can target multiple providers."],1],["cp16",0,2,"In the cloud data lifecycle, when should data FIRST be classified?",["Store","Use","Share","Create"],3,"Classification should be applied at creation (including modification) so every later phase can use the label to choose controls.","Domain 2",["Store follows create; waiting until storage leaves data briefly unlabeled and uncontrolled.","Use is too late; data may already be stored and shared without the right controls.","Share is later still; unclassified data could be shared inappropriately before anyone labels it.",null],2],["cp17",0,2,"An online store wants its order, analytics and support systems to stop handling real card numbers so they fall out of PCI DSS scope, while payments still work. Which technique BEST fits?",["Hashing each card number with SHA-256","Tokenization through a secured token vault","Static masking of the card numbers","Encrypting the card database with provider-managed keys"],1,"Tokens have no mathematical relationship to card numbers, and only the vault can map them back, so systems handling tokens can be removed from much of the PCI scope.","Domain 2",["Card numbers have limited possible values, so unsalted hashes can be brute-forced and hashing cannot be reversed for payments.",null,"Static masking permanently alters the data, so payments needing the real number would not work.","Encrypted card data is still cardholder data, and systems that can decrypt it remain in scope."],2],["cp18",0,2,"A development team needs a copy of production customer data for testing, without exposing real personal details to testers. Which approach is MOST appropriate?",["Dynamic data masking on the production database","Encrypting the test database with the production key","Static data masking of the test copy","Granting testers read-only access to production"],2,"Static masking creates a permanently masked copy that keeps realistic formats for testing while removing real values.","Domain 2",["Dynamic masking hides values at query time in production; testers would still work against production data.","Encryption protects data at rest, but testers need to read the data, so it would be decrypted for them.",null,"Read-only access to production still exposes real personal data to testers."],2],["cp19",0,2,"Which statement correctly distinguishes pseudonymized data from anonymized data?",["Pseudonymized data can be re-identified using separately held information","Anonymized data can be re-identified with the original key","Pseudonymized data falls outside privacy laws such as the GDPR because names are removed","Anonymized data must always be encrypted at rest"],0,"Pseudonymization replaces identifiers with codes that can be linked back; it remains personal data. Truly anonymized data cannot be linked back to a person.","Domain 2",[null,"Anonymized data, by definition, cannot be re-identified; a key that restores identity means it is only pseudonymized.","Pseudonymized data is still personal data under the GDPR because it can be re-identified.","Anonymized data is no longer personal data, so no encryption requirement follows from privacy law."],2],["cp20",0,2,"A regulator requires that a bank's cloud provider must never be able to decrypt certain records without the bank's live participation. Which key management approach meets this?",["Bring your own key imported into the provider's KMS","Provider-managed keys with automatic rotation","Customer-managed keys stored in the provider's KMS","Hold your own key (external key management)"],3,"HYOK keeps keys in the customer's own key service outside the provider, so the provider cannot decrypt without calling the customer's system.","Domain 2",["With BYOK the key is imported into the provider's KMS, so the provider's service can use it without the bank's participation.","Provider-managed keys are fully controlled by the provider, the opposite of the requirement.","Customer-managed keys still reside in the provider's KMS, where the provider's service can use them.",null],3],["cp21",0,2,"A code scan finds a database password in an application's configuration file committed to source control. What is the BEST long-term fix?",["Delete the file from the latest commit and keep using the same password afterwards","Base64-encode the password in the configuration file","Rotate the password and have the app fetch it at runtime from a secrets manager","Restrict the repository so only senior developers can read it"],2,"The exposed secret must be rotated, and retrieving it at runtime from a secrets manager (or using a workload identity) removes it from code entirely.","Domain 2",["The password remains in the repository history and is already exposed, so it must be rotated.","Base64 is an encoding, not encryption, and anyone can decode it.",null,"Limiting readers reduces exposure but still stores a live secret in source control."],1],["cp22",0,2,"Which storage type is lost when a cloud instance is stopped or terminated?",["Object storage","Ephemeral storage","Volume (block) storage","Long-term archival storage"],1,"Ephemeral storage is tied to the instance's life and disappears when the instance stops or terminates.","Domain 2",["Object storage is independent of any instance and persists until deleted.",null,"Block volumes are persistent and can be detached and reattached to other instances.","Archival storage is designed for durable long-term retention."],1],["cp23",0,2,"A weekly scan finds that one object storage bucket allows anonymous public reads. After removing the grant, what should the team do to prevent recurrence across the account?",["Rename the bucket so it is harder to guess","Move the bucket to a different region","Enable versioning on the bucket","Enable the account-level block on public access"],3,"An account-wide public access block stops any bucket from being made public by mistake, which addresses the root cause.","Domain 2",["Obscure names are not access control; bucket names are often discoverable.","Region does not affect whether a bucket policy allows public access.","Versioning protects against deletion and overwrites, not against public exposure.",null],2],["cp24",0,2,"Before deploying a cloud DLP solution, what should an organization do FIRST?",["Discover and classify its sensitive data","Enable blocking mode on all outbound channels","Deploy endpoint agents to all laptops","Enable TLS inspection at the network edge"],0,"DLP can only protect data it can recognize; discovery and classification define what to look for and how to treat it.","Domain 2",[null,"Blocking without classification produces many false positives and disrupts legitimate work.","Endpoint agents need policies based on classification to be useful.","TLS inspection gives visibility of traffic but still needs defined sensitive data to act on."],2],["cp25",0,2,"Remote employees upload files from home laptops directly to an unsanctioned SaaS app. Why might the company's network DLP appliance miss this?",["The traffic never passes through the corporate network","Network DLP appliances are only able to inspect outbound email traffic","SaaS applications do not use HTTPS","Network DLP only scans data at rest"],0,"Network DLP sees only traffic that crosses its inspection point; remote users going straight to the internet bypass it. Endpoint or API-based DLP and a CASB close the gap.","Domain 2",[null,"Network DLP can inspect many protocols, not only email.","SaaS apps almost always use HTTPS; that would make inspection harder, not explain why traffic is unseen.","Network DLP inspects data in motion; scanning data at rest is a different DLP type."],2],["cp26",0,2,"A company emailed a confidential design document to a partner who has since left the project. How can the company stop that person from opening copies already downloaded?",["Delete the document from the company's file share","Revoke the person's rights in the IRM system protecting the document","Enable DLP rules that block outbound email attachments to the partner's domain","Change the file share permissions to owner-only"],1,"IRM protection travels with the file; the client must obtain rights from the policy server, so revoking rights blocks access to existing copies.","Domain 2",["Deleting the original does nothing to copies the partner already has.",null,"Outbound DLP can stop future sending but cannot reach a copy already delivered.","Share permissions control the original location, not files already outside it."],2],["cp27",0,2,"Which is a recognized objective of information rights management (IRM)?",["Faster retrieval from archival storage","Automatic deduplication of documents","Persistent protection that follows the data","Replication of documents across regions"],2,"IRM objectives include persistent protection, dynamic policy control, automatic expiration, continuous auditing and interoperability.","Domain 2",["Retrieval speed is a storage performance property, not an IRM objective.","Deduplication is a storage efficiency feature unrelated to usage rights.",null,"Replication supports availability, not control of how content is used."],1],["cp28",0,2,"A lifecycle rule is about to delete chat records whose retention period has expired, but legal counsel has placed them under a litigation hold. What should happen?",["Delete them because the retention period has ended","Archive them and delete the originals on schedule","Delete them after exporting a summary for counsel","Preserve the records until counsel releases the hold"],3,"A legal hold overrides the normal retention schedule; deleting held data can lead to court sanctions.","Domain 2",["Retention periods do not apply while a hold is in force; deletion would violate the preservation duty.","Deleting originals on schedule can destroy metadata and evidence covered by the hold.","A summary is not a substitute for preserving the original records.",null],1],["cp29",0,2,"What must an archiving policy address to make sure encrypted archives can be read at the end of a ten-year retention period?",["Moving the archives to a new region every year to refresh the storage media","Compressing the archives to reduce storage cost","Retention and protection of the decryption keys for the full period","Storing archives in the same account as production"],2,"Archived encrypted data is useless without its keys, so key retention must match the data retention period.","Domain 2",["Moving regions annually adds risk and cost and does nothing for readability.","Compression saves cost but does not help decrypt the data later.",null,"Co-locating archives with production increases risk and does not address key availability."],3],["cp30",0,2,"An investigation cannot determine which engineer deleted a storage bucket because the team shares one administrator account. Which improvement BEST restores accountability?",["Longer retention of management plane logs","Stronger password for the shared administrator account","More frequent backups of the bucket contents","Unique federated identities for each administrator"],3,"Accountability requires attributing each action to an individual; unique identities make logs show who acted.","Domain 2",["Keeping logs longer does not help if every entry shows the same shared account.","A stronger password protects the account but still cannot distinguish between the people using it.","Backups aid recovery but do not show who performed the deletion.",null],1],["cp31",0,2,"Which data type is typically the HARDEST to discover and classify automatically?",["Structured data in relational tables","Unstructured data such as documents and chat messages","Semi-structured JSON records with nested keys and values","Database schemas and column names"],1,"Unstructured data has no schema or keys to read, so discovery depends on inspecting content.","Domain 2",["Structured data has schemas and column names that make discovery comparatively easy.",null,"JSON has keys and values that tools can parse, making it easier than free text.","Schemas and column names are metadata that directly help discovery."],1],["cp32",0,2,"Who should decide the classification level of a customer database?",["The data owner","The data custodian","The cloud service provider","The database administrator"],0,"The data owner is the business role accountable for the data and decides its classification and access.","Domain 2",[null,"The custodian implements controls on the owner's behalf but does not decide classification.","The provider operates infrastructure and has no authority over the customer's data classification.","The DBA is a custodian role that maintains the database, not the accountable owner."],1],["cp33",0,2,"A fraud model starts approving transactions that contain a specific unusual merchant code. Investigators find crafted records were added to its training data. Which control would MOST directly have prevented this?",["Encrypting the model endpoint's TLS traffic","Restricting write access to training data and validating data provenance","Rate-limiting and authenticating every query sent to the deployed model endpoint","Adding more GPU capacity for retraining"],1,"This is data poisoning with a backdoor trigger; controlling who can modify training data and verifying its origin and integrity addresses it.","Domain 2",["TLS protects prompts in transit but does not stop poisoned records entering training data.",null,"Rate limiting slows model extraction, not the tampering of training data.","More compute does nothing to ensure the integrity of the training set."],3],["cp34",0,3,"A bank's core system cannot be shut down for maintenance windows. What is the LOWEST Uptime Institute tier that allows planned maintenance of any component without shutting down IT equipment?",["Tier I","Tier II","Tier IV","Tier III"],3,"Tier III is concurrently maintainable, with multiple distribution paths. Tier IV adds fault tolerance but is not the lowest tier meeting the need.","Domain 3",["Tier I has single paths and no redundancy, so maintenance requires shutdown.","Tier II adds redundant components but still has a single distribution path that forces shutdowns for some work.","Tier IV also allows it, but it is not the lowest tier and costs more than needed.",null],3],["cp35",0,3,"Why do cloud providers use type 1 hypervisors rather than type 2 hypervisors for multitenant hosts?",["Type 1 lets tenants install their own host operating system","Type 1 does not require any patching","Type 1 runs directly on hardware with a smaller attack surface","Type 1 removes the need for tenant isolation"],2,"A bare-metal hypervisor avoids a general-purpose host OS, reducing attack surface and improving performance.","Domain 3",["Tenants never install the host OS; type 1 designs have no general-purpose host OS at all.","All hypervisors need patching; minimal code reduces but does not eliminate flaws.",null,"Isolation is the core purpose of the hypervisor, not something it makes unnecessary."],2],["cp36",0,3,"According to cloud breach data, which is the MOST common cause of cloud data exposure?",["Customer misconfiguration of cloud resources","VM escape through hypervisor flaws","Side-channel attacks between tenants","Physical theft of provider hardware"],0,"Misconfigurations such as public storage and over-broad permissions cause far more incidents than sophisticated infrastructure attacks.","Domain 3",[null,"VM escape is severe but rare in practice.","Side-channel attacks are real but seldom the cause of actual data exposures.","Provider data centers have strong physical controls, and physical theft is a very rare cause."],1],["cp37",0,3,"Researchers have launched hundreds of cloud instances, many with no owner and months of missing patches. Which risk does this describe?",["VM escape","VM sprawl","Hypervisor hyperjacking","Resource exhaustion by a neighbor"],1,"VM sprawl is uncontrolled growth of unmanaged virtual machines, which leaves unpatched, unowned systems exposed.","Domain 3",["VM escape is breaking out of a VM to the hypervisor, which the scenario does not describe.",null,"Hyperjacking is taking control of the hypervisor itself, not an accumulation of unmanaged guests.","A noisy neighbor consumes shared resources; the scenario is about unmanaged instances."],1],["cp38",0,3,"How does a public cloud customer normally gain assurance about the provider's data center physical security?",["By performing its own on-site inspection of each data center","By installing its own cameras in the provider's facility","By reviewing independent audit reports and certifications","By requiring the provider to share the badge access logs"],2,"Large providers do not allow individual customer visits; independent reports such as SOC 2 Type II and ISO/IEC 27001 cover physical controls.","Domain 3",["Public providers generally do not permit individual customer inspections of multitenant data centers.","Customers cannot place their own equipment for monitoring in a provider's shared facility.",null,"Raw access logs of shared facilities include other parties' data and are not provided to customers."],2],["cp39",0,3,"Eight engineers log in daily with the cloud account's root credentials. What should be done FIRST?",["Move daily work to federated admin roles and lock away the root credentials","Change the root password every month and share it with the engineers through a password vault","Enable detailed billing reports for the account","Add a second root account for redundancy"],0,"Root credentials should not be used for routine work; individual federated roles with MFA give least privilege and accountability, while root is secured and monitored.","Domain 3",[null,"Rotating a shared root password still leaves everyone using an all-powerful shared identity.","Billing reports do not address the misuse of privileged credentials.","Adding more all-powerful accounts increases risk instead of reducing it."],2],["cp40",0,3,"Return traffic to a web server is being blocked even though inbound HTTPS is allowed at the subnet level. What is the MOST likely cause?",["A stateful security group is dropping replies","The TLS certificate on the server has expired","The route table has no internet gateway route for inbound traffic","A stateless network ACL lacks an outbound rule"],3,"Network ACLs are usually stateless, so both directions must be allowed. Security groups are stateful and permit return traffic automatically.","Domain 3",["Stateful security groups automatically allow responses to permitted inbound traffic.","An expired certificate causes TLS errors, not network-level blocking of return traffic.","Inbound traffic is already reaching the server, so routing for inbound is not the problem.",null],3],["cp41",0,3,"After a breach, an attacker moved from a compromised web server to database servers in the same subnet. Which control would BEST limit this lateral movement?",["Microsegmentation between workloads","A larger subnet address range","A web application firewall in front of the site","Longer retention of flow logs"],0,"Microsegmentation restricts communication between individual workloads to only required paths, even within one subnet.","Domain 3",[null,"Subnet size does not restrict traffic between hosts within it.","A WAF filters inbound web requests but does not control server-to-server traffic.","Flow logs help investigation but do not prevent movement."],2],["cp42",0,3,"Which principle is central to a zero trust architecture?",["Trust all traffic that originates inside the virtual network","Verify every request based on identity and context","Rely on the VPN as the single security boundary","Allow administrators unrestricted standing access"],1,"Zero trust removes implicit trust based on network location and verifies identity, device and context for every access, with least privilege.","Domain 3",["Implicit trust for internal traffic is exactly what zero trust rejects.",null,"A single perimeter boundary such as a VPN is the model zero trust replaces.","Standing unrestricted access violates least privilege, a core zero trust tenet."],1],["cp43",0,3,"A workload has an RTO of 4 hours and an RPO of 1 hour, and budget is tight. Which DR strategy is MOST appropriate?",["Active-active deployment across two regions running at full capacity","Weekly backups restored manually after a disaster","No DR plan, relying on the provider's SLA credits","Pilot light in a second region with hourly replication"],3,"Pilot light keeps core data replicated and minimal systems ready to scale, meeting a 4-hour RTO and 1-hour RPO at modest cost.","Domain 3",["Active-active meets the targets but at far higher cost than needed.","Weekly backups cannot meet a one-hour RPO and manual restore may exceed four hours.","SLA credits compensate financially but do not recover the service.",null],2],["cp44",0,3,"Which DR test actually fails over production systems to the recovery site and carries the MOST risk?",["Tabletop exercise","Parallel test","Full interruption test","Checklist review"],2,"A full interruption test shuts down or fails over production, proving recovery but risking real outage.","Domain 3",["A tabletop exercise is a discussion and does not touch systems.","A parallel test recovers systems at the alternate site without stopping production.",null,"A checklist review only verifies that plan documents are complete."],2],["cp45",0,3,"An IaaS customer needs packet-level visibility of traffic to its own instances for an investigation. Which option is available?",["The provider's traffic mirroring feature for the instances' interfaces","A physical tap on the provider's top-of-rack switch","Promiscuous mode to capture neighbors' traffic","Requesting full packet captures of the provider's backbone network for the incident window"],0,"Customers cannot touch physical networks; provider traffic mirroring or host-based capture on their own instances gives packet visibility.","Domain 3",[null,"Customers have no access to the provider's physical switches.","Tenant isolation prevents seeing other tenants' traffic, and trying to would be inappropriate.","Providers do not hand out backbone captures, which contain other customers' data."],2],["cp46",0,3,"A SIEM alert links a sign-in from a new country, creation of a new access key and a large storage download within 15 minutes. Which audit mechanism produced this insight?",["Packet capture","Correlation","Log retention","Flow sampling"],1,"Correlation links related events from several sources to reveal a pattern that no single event shows.","Domain 3",["Packet capture records traffic contents; it does not link identity and storage events.",null,"Retention keeps logs available but does not analyze relationships between them.","Flow sampling reduces network data volume; it does not tie together events from different services."],2],["cp47",0,3,"Which item is a physical and environmental protection control in a data center?",["Security groups on virtual machines","Encryption of data in transit with TLS","A mantrap at the entrance to the server hall","Federated single sign-on for administrators"],2,"A mantrap physically controls entry so only one authenticated person passes at a time.","Domain 3",["Security groups are logical network controls, not physical ones.","TLS is a system and communications protection control.",null,"Single sign-on is an identification and authentication control."],1],["cp48",0,3,"In software-defined networking, what does the control plane do?",["Physically carries packets between hosts","Encrypts all tenant traffic","Stores the tenants' virtual machine images","Decides how traffic should be forwarded"],3,"SDN separates the control plane, which makes forwarding decisions and can be programmed via APIs, from the data plane, which forwards packets.","Domain 3",["Carrying packets is the job of the data plane.","Encryption is a separate function and not the definition of the control plane.","Image storage is a storage service, unrelated to network control.",null],2],["cp49",0,4,"During design review, a team finds that a user could upload files that appear to come from another user. Which STRIDE category is this?",["Tampering","Repudiation","Spoofing","Elevation of privilege"],2,"Spoofing is pretending to be someone else; it violates authentication.","Domain 4",["Tampering is unauthorized modification of data, not impersonation.","Repudiation is denying having performed an action, which relates to missing audit evidence.",null,"Elevation of privilege is gaining rights beyond those granted."],2],["cp50",0,4,"Which threat modeling method is a seven-stage, risk-centric process that starts from business objectives?",["STRIDE","DREAD","ATASM","PASTA"],3,"PASTA (Process for Attack Simulation and Threat Analysis) has seven stages beginning with business objectives and ending with risk and impact analysis.","Domain 4",["STRIDE is a classification of threat types, not a seven-stage process.","DREAD is a scoring model for rating threats.","ATASM is a four-step sequence: architecture, threats, attack surfaces, mitigations.",null],2],["cp51",0,4,"An image-preview feature fetches any URL a user supplies. A tester uses it to read temporary credentials from the instance metadata service. What vulnerability is this?",["Cross-site scripting (XSS)","Server-side request forgery (SSRF)","Cross-site request forgery (CSRF)","SQL injection"],1,"SSRF makes a server send requests to attacker-chosen destinations, such as the metadata endpoint, which is a classic cloud credential theft path.","Domain 4",["XSS injects script into pages viewed by other users; it does not make the server fetch internal URLs.",null,"CSRF abuses a user's browser to send unwanted requests to a trusted site.","SQL injection targets database queries, not outbound server requests."],2],["cp52",0,4,"Which testing approach analyzes source code for security flaws without executing the application?",["Static application security testing (SAST)","Dynamic application security testing (DAST)","Interactive application security testing (IAST)","Load and performance testing"],0,"SAST examines code at rest early in development and points to specific lines.","Domain 4",[null,"DAST tests a running application from outside.","IAST uses an agent inside a running application during tests.","Load testing measures performance under demand, not code flaws."],1],["cp53",0,4,"A critical vulnerability is announced in a popular open-source logging library. Which tool helps a company find which of its services include it?",["A web application firewall","Dynamic application security testing","A network vulnerability scanner","Software composition analysis with SBOMs"],3,"SCA inventories third-party components and, with software bills of materials, shows exactly where a library is used.","Domain 4",["A WAF may block some exploit attempts but does not inventory components.","DAST tests running apps from outside and may miss a library that is not reachable.","Network scanners look at hosts and ports, not libraries inside application builds.",null],1],["cp54",0,4,"Which OWASP ASVS level is recommended for most applications that handle sensitive data?",["Level 2","Level 1","Level 3","Level 0"],0,"Level 2 is the recommended standard level for most applications with sensitive data; Level 3 is for the most critical systems.","Domain 4",[null,"Level 1 is a basic minimum suitable for all applications, not the recommended level for sensitive data.","Level 3 is reserved for the most critical, high-assurance applications.","The ASVS defines levels 1 to 3; there is no Level 0 verification level."],3],["cp55",0,4,"What is the MOST effective defense against SQL injection in a cloud-hosted application?",["A web application firewall only","Parameterized queries","Encrypting the database at rest","Hiding detailed error messages"],1,"Parameterized queries keep user input separate from SQL code, removing the root cause. A WAF is a compensating control.","Domain 4",["A WAF can block known patterns but can be bypassed and does not fix the code.",null,"Encryption at rest does not stop the application from executing injected queries.","Hiding errors makes exploitation harder to see but leaves the flaw in place."],1],["cp56",0,4,"A malicious public package with the same name as a company's internal library gets pulled into a build. Which control would BEST prevent this?",["Running DAST against the staging environment","Adding a WAF in front of the build server","Pulling packages only from a curated internal repository","Increasing peer code review of the team's own application logic"],2,"This is dependency confusion; resolving packages only through a curated internal repository or proxy, with pinned versions, prevents public look-alikes being fetched.","Domain 4",["DAST tests a running app and would not stop a malicious dependency being built in.","A WAF protects web apps from inbound requests, not package resolution during builds.",null,"Reviewing the team's own logic does not inspect how dependencies are resolved."],3],["cp57",0,4,"Partners call a company's REST APIs. The company needs per-partner quotas, token validation and central logging. Which component fits BEST?",["Database activity monitor","XML firewall","API gateway","Application sandbox"],2,"An API gateway authenticates callers, enforces quotas and rate limits, routes requests and logs traffic in one place.","Domain 4",["DAM monitors database queries, not API authentication and quotas.","An XML firewall validates XML messages; it is not the general front door for REST APIs with quotas.",null,"A sandbox isolates untrusted code; it does not manage API access."],2],["cp58",0,4,"A service account that normally reads a few rows starts reading an entire customer table at 3 a.m. Which control is designed to detect this?",["Web application firewall","Database activity monitoring","TLS certificate pinning","Static code analysis"],1,"DAM watches database queries and alerts on unusual activity, including misuse by authorized accounts.","Domain 4",["A WAF inspects HTTP traffic and would not see direct database queries.",null,"Certificate pinning protects connections from interception, not misuse of valid access.","Static analysis reviews code, not runtime query behavior."],2],["cp59",0,4,"Which statement about OAuth 2.0 is correct?",["It is an authorization framework for delegated API access","It is an XML standard for enterprise single sign-on","It authenticates users by itself without any identity layer","It replaces the need for TLS between client and server"],0,"OAuth 2.0 lets an app obtain limited access to an API on a user's behalf. OpenID Connect adds authentication on top; SAML is the XML SSO standard.","Domain 4",[null,"That describes SAML 2.0, not OAuth.","OAuth alone does not define user authentication; OpenID Connect adds that layer.","OAuth relies on TLS to protect tokens in transit."],2],["cp60",0,4,"A security team wants to discover which unsanctioned cloud applications employees use and apply data policies to them. Which tool is designed for this?",["Hardware security module (HSM)","Database activity monitor","Identity provider","Cloud access security broker (CASB)"],3,"A CASB gives visibility into cloud app use, including shadow IT, and enforces access, DLP and threat policies.","Domain 4",["An HSM protects cryptographic keys; it does not discover cloud app usage.","A DAM monitors database queries, not SaaS usage by employees.","An IdP authenticates users to federated apps but does not discover unsanctioned apps.",null],1],["cp61",0,4,"In which SDLC phase does threat modeling provide the MOST value?",["Deployment","Design","Operations","Disposal"],1,"Threat modeling in design finds architectural flaws when they are cheapest to fix, before code exists.","Domain 4",["By deployment, design flaws are built in and expensive to change.",null,"In operations, fixing design flaws may require redesign and emergency releases.","At disposal, the system is being retired and threat modeling offers little benefit."],1],["cp62",0,4,"Before running a penetration test against its own workloads hosted on a public cloud, what should the customer do FIRST?",["Test the provider's hypervisor for escape flaws","Scan other tenants' IP ranges for comparison","Disable logging to avoid alert noise","Review the provider's penetration testing policy"],3,"Providers set rules on what customers may test; following them avoids violating terms or disrupting shared infrastructure.","Domain 4",["Attacking the provider's underlying platform is almost always prohibited.","Scanning other tenants is unauthorized and potentially illegal.","Disabling logging removes evidence and monitoring; tests should be coordinated, not hidden.",null],2],["cp63",0,5,"Which hardware component allows a host to prove that it booted with approved firmware and software?",["Trusted platform module (TPM)","Baseboard management controller","Network interface card","Hardware load balancer"],0,"A TPM records boot measurements, supporting secure and measured boot and remote attestation.","Domain 5",[null,"A BMC provides out-of-band management; it is a target to protect, not a boot integrity anchor.","A network card moves traffic and has no role in measuring the boot process.","A load balancer distributes traffic and does not attest to host boot integrity."],1],["cp64",0,5,"A platform team wants every new VM to start from a patched, hardened and scanned baseline. What should it implement?",["Manual hardening checklists for each new VM","Snapshots of production VMs copied to new projects","A catalog of signed golden images","Default marketplace images used as provided"],2,"Golden images built to a benchmark, scanned, signed and published to an approved catalog give consistent secure starting points.","Domain 5",["Manual checklists are slow and inconsistent compared with pre-built images.","Copying production snapshots spreads data and secrets and does not guarantee hardening.",null,"Unmodified marketplace images may not meet the organization's hardening baseline."],2],["cp65",0,5,"What does DNSSEC provide?",["Confidentiality of DNS queries between clients and resolvers","Load balancing of DNS servers","Automatic IP address assignment","Integrity and authenticity of DNS responses"],3,"DNSSEC signs DNS records so resolvers can verify they are authentic and unaltered, defending against cache poisoning. It does not encrypt queries.","Domain 5",["DNSSEC does not encrypt queries; encrypted DNS transports address confidentiality.","Load balancing is unrelated to DNSSEC's signing function.","Automatic address assignment is DHCP's job.",null],1],["cp66",0,5,"An operations team wants administrators to reach private cloud servers without any inbound SSH port open to the internet. Which approach BEST achieves this?",["A provider-managed session service with SSO and MFA","Opening SSH to the internet with a strong password","Changing SSH to a non-standard port","Sharing one SSH key among all administrators"],0,"Managed session services broker access through the management plane without open inbound ports, with centralized authentication and session logging.","Domain 5",[null,"Internet-exposed SSH remains an attack target regardless of password strength.","A non-standard port is obscurity; scanners still find it.","A shared key destroys individual accountability and is hard to revoke."],2],["cp67",0,5,"An engineer manually opened a firewall rule in the console, so the live environment no longer matches its Terraform code. What is this called?",["Immutable infrastructure","Configuration drift","Blue-green deployment","Policy as code"],1,"Drift is divergence between the actual configuration and the approved baseline or code, often from manual changes that bypass review.","Domain 5",["Immutable infrastructure is the practice that prevents in-place changes like this.",null,"Blue-green deployment is a release strategy that switches between two environments.","Policy as code enforces rules automatically; it is a control against drift, not the drift itself."],1],["cp68",0,5,"In a managed PaaS database service, who is responsible for patching the database engine?",["The customer's database administrator","The customer's application developers","The cloud provider","An independent third-party auditor"],2,"In PaaS the provider maintains the platform, including the database engine; the customer manages configuration, access and data.","Domain 5",["Customers do not have access to patch the engine in a managed service.","Developers manage application code, not the managed platform underneath.",null,"Auditors assess controls; they do not operate or patch systems."],1],["cp69",0,5,"A critical patch cannot be applied to a legacy application server for three months. What is the BEST course of action?",["Leave the server unchanged until the vendor eventually offers a supported replacement","Apply the patch directly in production without testing","Document an exception with compensating controls and a remediation date","Remove the server from the asset inventory"],2,"An approved, time-limited exception with compensating controls such as isolation and extra monitoring manages the risk until remediation.","Domain 5",["Doing nothing leaves an undocumented, unmanaged risk.","Untested production patching risks outages; the scenario says the patch cannot be applied yet.",null,"Hiding the asset from inventory removes visibility and makes the risk worse."],2],["cp70",0,5,"What BEST proves that cloud backups will support recovery within the RTO?",["Regular test restores measured against the RTO","Increasing the backup frequency to every hour","Storing backups in a second region","Encrypting backups with customer-managed keys"],0,"Only restoring and timing it proves recovery works and meets the objective.","Domain 5",[null,"More frequent backups improve RPO but prove nothing about restore time.","A second region protects against regional loss but does not show restores work.","Encryption protects confidentiality, not recoverability."],2],["cp71",0,5,"An application fails to scale during a traffic spike because the account reached its limit on running instances. What should capacity monitoring have included?",["Hypervisor firmware versions","Service quotas and account limits","TLS cipher suite settings","Data classification labels"],1,"In the cloud, quotas can stop scaling as surely as hardware shortages, so they belong in capacity monitoring and planning.","Domain 5",["Customers do not manage hypervisor firmware, and it does not limit instance counts.",null,"Cipher suites affect security of connections, not the number of instances allowed.","Classification labels guide data handling, not capacity."],2],["cp72",0,5,"Three outages in a month were caused by expired certificates. Service was restored quickly each time. Which ITIL process should now find and remove the root cause?",["Incident management","Release management","Service level management","Problem management"],3,"Problem management identifies and eliminates root causes of recurring incidents; incident management restores service.","Domain 5",["Incident management focuses on restoring service fast, which already happened each time.","Release management moves changes into production; it does not investigate root causes.","Service level management agrees and tracks targets, not root-cause analysis.",null],2],["cp73",0,5,"What is the primary purpose of a configuration management database (CMDB) during change management?",["Storing application secrets and passwords used during automated deployment","Recording customer support tickets","Holding encrypted backups of servers","Showing configuration items and dependencies to assess impact"],3,"A CMDB records configuration items and their relationships, which lets teams judge what a change will affect.","Domain 5",["Secrets belong in a secrets manager, not a CMDB.","Support tickets live in a service desk tool, not in the CMDB.","Backups are stored in backup systems, not the CMDB.",null],2],["cp74",0,5,"A web server in IaaS shows signs of compromise. What should the responder do FIRST to preserve evidence while stopping the attack?",["Isolate it with a restrictive security group, then capture memory and snapshot disks","Terminate the instance immediately and launch a clean replacement from the golden image","Reboot the instance to clear the attacker's processes","Restore the instance from last night's backup"],0,"Isolation contains the threat without destroying volatile evidence; memory and disk captures preserve it for analysis.","Domain 5",[null,"Terminating destroys memory and any ephemeral storage before they are captured.","Rebooting wipes volatile evidence such as running processes and memory.","Restoring overwrites the compromised state before it has been preserved."],3],["cp75",0,5,"Which document records who collected each piece of digital evidence and everyone who handled it afterwards?",["Rules of engagement","Chain of custody","Business impact analysis","Service level agreement"],1,"The chain of custody is the unbroken record of collection, handling, transfer and storage that makes evidence trustworthy.","Domain 5",["Rules of engagement define the scope and limits of a security test.",null,"A BIA identifies critical processes and recovery objectives.","An SLA defines service commitments between provider and customer."],1],["cp76",0,5,"During an incident affecting customer data, a technical engineer is contacted by a journalist. What should the engineer do?",["Explain the technical details of the attack to correct any rumors quickly","Confirm the number of affected customers","Refer the journalist to the designated communications function","Deny that any incident has occurred"],2,"Communication follows the communication plan and authority; public statements come from designated spokespeople coordinated with legal.","Domain 5",["Unauthorized technical disclosure can breach legal and contractual duties and harm the investigation.","Confirming figures is for authorized spokespeople after legal review.",null,"Denying a real incident is dishonest and can create legal liability."],1],["cp77",0,5,"A SOAR playbook detects an access key used from an anonymizing network to list storage buckets. What should the automated containment step be?",["Delete all storage buckets in the account","Disable the compromised access key","Shut down the SIEM to reduce alert noise","Rotate the TLS certificates on the web servers"],1,"Revoking or disabling compromised credentials is the typical first containment action in the cloud.","Domain 5",["Deleting buckets destroys data and evidence and is not proportionate.",null,"Disabling the SIEM removes visibility during an active incident.","TLS certificates are unrelated to a stolen access key."],2],["cp78",0,5,"What is a key limitation of ML-based anomaly detection in security operations?",["It can only detect known malware signatures","It cannot process cloud audit logs","It never produces false positives","Slow attackers can blend into the learned baseline"],3,"Anomaly models learn normal behavior; patient attackers acting within normal patterns, or activity present during training, can go undetected.","Domain 5",["Signature matching is a different technique; anomaly detection aims at unknown behavior.","Cloud audit logs are a common input to anomaly detection services.","Anomaly detection commonly produces false positives when legitimate behavior changes.",null],2],["cp79",0,6,"A European company stores data only in EU regions of a provider headquartered in another country. Why might foreign law still reach that data?",["The provider may be compelled by its home country's laws","EU regions are legally part of the provider's home country","Data in the cloud is not subject to any national law","Encryption at rest automatically transfers jurisdiction"],0,"A provider can be subject to its home jurisdiction's legal orders for data it controls anywhere, creating conflicts with local privacy law.","Domain 6",[null,"Data centers in the EU are under EU member state jurisdiction, not the provider's home country.","Cloud data is subject to laws in several jurisdictions, not none.","Encryption does not change jurisdiction, though customer-held keys can limit what is disclosed."],2],["cp80",0,6,"Which international standard series provides guidance on electronic discovery?",["ISO/IEC 27037","ISO/IEC 27036","ISO/IEC 27050","ISO/IEC 31000"],2,"ISO/IEC 27050 covers eDiscovery: identification, preservation, collection, processing, review and production of electronically stored information.","Domain 6",["ISO/IEC 27037 covers identification, collection, acquisition and preservation of digital evidence.","ISO/IEC 27036 covers information security in supplier relationships.",null,"ISO/IEC 31000 gives general risk management guidelines."],2],["cp81",0,6,"A company uses a SaaS CRM to store its customers' personal data. Under the GDPR, what role does the SaaS provider usually play?",["Controller","Data subject","Supervisory authority","Processor"],3,"The company decides the purposes and means (controller); the provider processes data on its instructions (processor).","Domain 6",["The controller is the company that determines why and how data is processed.","Data subjects are the individuals the data describes.","The supervisory authority is the regulator that enforces the GDPR.",null],1],["cp82",0,6,"A US clinic plans to store patient records with a cloud provider. Which agreement does HIPAA require with that provider?",["Business associate agreement (BAA)","Master services agreement only","Standard contractual clauses","Non-disclosure agreement only"],0,"HIPAA requires a BAA with service providers that create, receive, maintain or transmit protected health information for a covered entity.","Domain 6",[null,"An MSA sets general terms but does not by itself contain the HIPAA-required obligations.","Standard contractual clauses are a GDPR mechanism for international transfers.","An NDA covers confidentiality but not the specific safeguards HIPAA requires."],1],["cp83",0,6,"When should a privacy impact assessment be performed for a new cloud-based HR system?",["After the first data breach","Before the system is implemented","Only during the annual external audit","When the system is decommissioned"],1,"A PIA identifies privacy risks early so they can be mitigated in design and contracts.","Domain 6",["Waiting for a breach defeats the preventive purpose of the assessment.",null,"Annual audits may review PIAs but are not when they should first be done.","At decommissioning it is too late to shape how data is collected and used."],1],["cp84",0,6,"A customer reviewing a provider's SOC 2 report sees a section listing controls the customer must operate itself. What is this section?",["Management's assertion","Subservice organization carve-out","Complementary user entity controls","Auditor's opinion letter"],2,"Complementary user entity controls are the customer-side controls needed for the provider's described controls to be effective.","Domain 6",["Management's assertion is the provider's statement about its system, not a list of customer duties.","A carve-out excludes a subservice organization's controls from the report's scope.",null,"The opinion letter gives the auditor's conclusion, not customer responsibilities."],2],["cp85",0,6,"A company buys cyber insurance to cover losses from a cloud provider outage. Which risk treatment is this, and what does it NOT transfer?",["Risk avoidance; the outage risk is eliminated","Risk acceptance; no action is taken","Risk transfer; accountability for the data and reputation remain","Risk mitigation; the likelihood of an outage at the provider is reduced"],2,"Insurance transfers financial impact, but accountability, regulatory duties and reputational harm stay with the organization.","Domain 6",["Avoidance means not doing the activity; insurance does not remove the outage risk.","Buying insurance is an action, so it is not simple acceptance.",null,"Insurance does not reduce the chance of an outage, so it is not mitigation."],3],["cp86",0,6,"Which NIST publication defines the Risk Management Framework that underpins FedRAMP authorizations?",["NIST SP 800-37","NIST SP 800-145","NIST SP 800-88","NIST SP 800-61"],0,"SP 800-37 defines the RMF steps: prepare, categorize, select, implement, assess, authorize and monitor.","Domain 6",[null,"SP 800-145 is the NIST definition of cloud computing.","SP 800-88 covers media sanitization.","SP 800-61 is the computer security incident handling guide."],2],["cp87",0,6,"A company is worried that leaving its SaaS provider in the future will be costly and could lose data. Which contract terms BEST address this?",["Higher service credits for missed availability","A longer initial contract term","A larger limitation of liability for the provider","Exit terms covering data export format, timing and deletion"],3,"Exit and portability terms guarantee data can be retrieved in a usable format and then deleted, reducing lock-in.","Domain 6",["Service credits compensate for downtime but do not help retrieve data on exit.","A longer term deepens commitment and does nothing for portability.","Liability limits concern damages, not the ability to leave with your data.",null],2],["cp88",0,6,"Which standard series addresses information security in supplier relationships, including a part specific to cloud services?",["ISO/IEC 27017","ISO/IEC 27036","ISO/IEC 20000-1","ISO/IEC 42001"],1,"ISO/IEC 27036 covers supplier relationship security across the lifecycle and includes guidance for cloud services.","Domain 6",["ISO/IEC 27017 gives cloud security controls but is not a supplier relationship standard.",null,"ISO/IEC 20000-1 specifies IT service management system requirements.","ISO/IEC 42001 specifies AI management system requirements."],3],["cp89",0,6,"A bank plans to use a cloud AI model to pre-screen loan applications. Which measure BEST addresses its ethical and regulatory obligations?",["Relying on the provider's model card as proof of fairness","Bias testing with human review of rejections","Hiding AI use from applicants to avoid complaints","Letting the model make final decisions to ensure consistency"],1,"High-risk AI uses call for bias testing, human oversight, transparency and accountability, which remain the bank's responsibility.","Domain 6",["A provider's documentation helps but does not prove fairness for the bank's own data and use.",null,"Transparency obligations generally require telling people when AI affects decisions about them.","Removing human oversight from significant decisions conflicts with accountability expectations."],3],["cp90",0,6,"A retailer assumes its checkout is PCI DSS compliant because its cloud provider holds a PCI DSS attestation. What must the retailer verify?",["That the provider's attestation is dated within the last five years and is signed","That the provider also holds a SOC 3 report","That its services are in the provider's scope and its own controls comply","That its payment pages use a content delivery network"],2,"A provider's attestation covers only listed services and the provider's responsibilities; the customer's configuration and processes need their own compliance evidence.","Domain 6",["PCI DSS validation is annual, and age alone says nothing about scope or customer controls.","A SOC 3 report is a general summary and does not establish PCI DSS compliance.",null,"A CDN is a performance choice and does not demonstrate compliance."],2],["x1",0,1,"An online retailer's cloud application automatically adds servers during a holiday sale and removes them when traffic drops, so it pays only for what it uses. Which NIST essential characteristic does the automatic scaling show?",["Broad network access","Resource pooling","On-demand self-service","Rapid elasticity"],3,"Rapid elasticity is the ability to scale capacity out and in quickly, often automatically, to match demand. On-demand self-service is about consumers provisioning resources without provider interaction, not about automatic scaling with load.","Domain 1: NIST essential characteristics",["Broad network access means services are reachable over standard networks from many kinds of devices.","Resource pooling is the provider serving many tenants from shared resources, not scaling one workload.","Self-service is about consumers provisioning resources without provider help, not automatic scaling with load.",null],1],["x2",0,1,"A regulator requires an independent firm to examine a cloud provider's controls and report on them. In the NIST cloud reference architecture, which role does this firm play?",["Cloud auditor","Cloud broker","Cloud carrier","Cloud consumer"],0,"The cloud auditor conducts independent assessments of cloud services, security controls and performance. A cloud broker manages or integrates services for consumers but does not provide independent assurance.","Domain 1: Cloud roles",[null,"A broker negotiates, aggregates or integrates services; it does not give independent assurance.","A carrier provides the network connectivity and transport between consumers and providers.","The consumer uses the service; the auditor is the independent party that assesses it."],1],["x3",0,1,"A company runs its workloads in its own data center but sends overflow processing to a public cloud during month-end peaks. Which deployment model and practice does this describe?",["Hybrid cloud with cloud bursting","Community cloud with shared tenancy","Private cloud with rapid elasticity","Multi-cloud with provider redundancy"],0,"Using an on-premises or private environment and moving excess load to a public cloud when needed is cloud bursting in a hybrid model. Multi-cloud means using several public providers, which is not what happens here.","Domain 1: Deployment models",[null,"A community cloud is shared by organizations with common needs; the scenario has one company.","A private cloud alone would not send overflow work to a public provider.","Multi-cloud means using more than one provider; here there is one data center plus one public cloud."],1],["x4",0,1,"A team moves an API to serverless functions. Which security responsibility stays with the customer?",["Patching the operating system of the servers that run the functions","Hardening the hypervisor that isolates tenants' function runtimes","Secure function code and least-privilege function permissions","Physical security of the facilities that host the function platform"],2,"In serverless, the provider manages servers, operating systems and runtime infrastructure, while the customer remains responsible for its code, data, configuration and permissions. OS patching is the tempting choice, but it moves to the provider.","Domain 1: Shared responsibility for serverless",["The provider patches the operating systems under serverless functions.","The virtualization layer and tenant isolation are the provider's job.",null,"Physical security is always the provider's responsibility in public cloud."],2],["x5",0,1,"A company wants to be able to move its virtual machine images and data to another provider later without rewriting them. Which property should it evaluate?",["Interoperability","Reversibility of contracts","Multitenancy","Portability"],3,"Portability is the ability to move applications and data between providers with little rework. Interoperability is the ability of systems to work together and exchange data, which is related but not the same as moving workloads.","Domain 1: Portability and interoperability",["Interoperability is about systems working together and exchanging data, not moving workloads.","Reversibility covers getting data back and exiting a contract, not moving VM images unchanged.","Multitenancy is sharing infrastructure among customers and says nothing about moving workloads.",null],2],["x6",0,1,"A government agency stores records in the cloud that must stay confidential for 25 years. Why should it plan now for post-quantum cryptography?",["Quantum computers can already break AES-256 in minutes","Post-quantum algorithms make key management unnecessary","Encrypted data captured today could be decrypted later by a capable quantum computer","Cloud providers plan to stop supporting TLS connections to their storage services within the next year"],2,"The 'harvest now, decrypt later' risk means long-lived confidential data encrypted with today's public-key algorithms may be exposed once large quantum computers exist. AES-256 is considered resistant; the concern is mainly RSA and elliptic-curve key exchange.","Domain 1: Quantum and cryptography",["No quantum computer can break AES-256 today; symmetric keys of that size are considered quantum-resistant.","Post-quantum algorithms still need keys to be generated, stored, rotated and protected.",null,"TLS is not being dropped; it is being extended with post-quantum key exchange."],3],["x7",0,1,"A utility deploys edge computing nodes in unstaffed substations to process sensor data locally. Which security concern is MORE significant for these nodes than for servers in a provider's data center?",["Hypervisor escape between cloud tenants","Lack of any network connectivity to the core cloud","Physical tampering and theft of the devices","Higher latency for local sensor processing"],2,"Edge nodes sit in locations without the physical controls of a data center, so tampering, theft and data extraction from devices become major risks, which calls for encryption, secure boot and tamper detection. Latency is actually lower at the edge, which is why edge is used.","Domain 1: Edge computing",["Hypervisor escape is a multitenant data center concern, not the distinctive edge risk.","Edge nodes usually do connect back to the cloud, even if intermittently.",null,"Edge processing is used to reduce latency, so higher latency is not the concern."],2],["x8",0,1,"Two providers both appear in the CSA STAR registry. Provider A has a self-assessment; provider B has a third-party certification. What does provider B's entry give a customer that provider A's does not?",["A guarantee that the provider has never had a breach","Proof that the provider meets HIPAA requirements","Legal transfer of the customer's compliance obligations","Independent verification of the provider's controls"],3,"STAR Level 1 is a self-assessment, while Level 2 involves an independent third-party audit or certification, which gives stronger assurance. No certification guarantees the absence of breaches.","Domain 1: CSA STAR",["No certification or audit can guarantee a provider has never been breached.","STAR is based on the CSA Cloud Controls Matrix and does not by itself prove HIPAA compliance.","Customers keep their compliance obligations; a provider certification does not transfer them.",null],3],["x9",0,1,"A vendor says its virtualization product is certified under Common Criteria at EAL4+. What does this rating actually tell the buyer?",["That the product cannot be compromised by any known attack","That the product's cryptographic modules are FIPS 140-3 validated","That the vendor's own cloud operations passed a SOC 2 Type II audit last year","How rigorously the product was evaluated against its security target"],3,"An Evaluation Assurance Level describes the depth and rigor of the evaluation against a defined security target, not how strong the product is against every attack. FIPS 140-3 is a separate validation program for cryptographic modules.","Domain 1: Common Criteria",["An EAL rating measures evaluation rigor; it never promises immunity to attacks.","FIPS 140-3 validation is a separate program for cryptographic modules.","SOC 2 reports cover an organization's operational controls, not a product's Common Criteria rating.",null],3],["x10",0,1,"A company connects a cloud-hosted AI assistant to its document repository using retrieval-augmented generation. Testers find the assistant quotes HR files to employees who have no access to them. What is the BEST fix?",["Fine-tune the model on the HR files so it answers HR questions more accurately for everyone","Increase the model's context window so fewer documents are skipped","Enforce the user's own permissions when retrieving documents for each query","Add a disclaimer to answers saying they may contain confidential data"],2,"The retrieval layer must only return documents the requesting user is allowed to read, so the model never sees content outside that user's permissions. Fine-tuning on HR files would embed the sensitive data in the model and make the leak worse.","Domain 1: AI security in the cloud",["Fine-tuning on sensitive files bakes the data into the model and worsens the exposure.","A larger context window retrieves more content and does nothing about authorization.",null,"A disclaimer does not stop unauthorized disclosure of HR data."],3],["x11",0,2,"A cloud storage service splits each object into fragments, adds parity fragments and spreads them across several data centers, so the object can be rebuilt even if one site is lost. What is this technique called?",["Tokenization with a central vault","Data masking at the storage layer","Data dispersion using erasure coding","Deduplication across availability zones"],2,"Data dispersion (bit splitting) with erasure coding breaks data into fragments plus parity so it survives the loss of some fragments, and no single site holds a complete copy. Deduplication removes duplicate copies to save space, the opposite of adding redundancy.","Domain 2: Data dispersion",["Tokenization replaces sensitive values with tokens; it does not fragment objects for resilience.","Masking hides values from viewers; it does not spread fragments across sites.",null,"Deduplication removes redundant copies to save space rather than adding recoverable parity."],3],["x12",0,2,"Call center agents must see only the last four digits of customers' card numbers on screen, while the billing service must still process the full number from the same database. Which technique fits BEST?",["Static masking of the production database","Hashing the card numbers with SHA-256","Deleting the card numbers after each transaction","Dynamic data masking based on the viewer's role"],3,"Dynamic masking changes what is displayed at query time depending on who is asking, while the stored value stays intact for authorized services. Static masking permanently alters a copy of the data, so billing could no longer use the real number.","Domain 2: Data masking",["Static masking permanently replaces values, so billing could not use the real number.","A hash cannot be reversed, so the billing service could not recover the card number.","Deleting the numbers would break billing, which still needs the full value.",null],2],["x13",0,2,"An analyst restores a file from cloud archive storage and must confirm it has not changed since it was archived five years ago. What should she compare?",["The file's size with the size shown in the storage console","A hash of the restored file with the hash recorded at archiving","The file's last-modified date with the archive date","The file's name with the name in the archive index"],1,"A cryptographic hash changes if even one bit of the file changes, so matching hashes show integrity. Size, dates and names can all stay the same even when the content has been altered.","Domain 2: Hashing for integrity",["A file can be altered without changing its size.",null,"Timestamps can be preserved or changed independently of the content.","The name says nothing about whether the content changed."],1],["x14",0,2,"A company is deploying a new cloud DLP tool. The business fears it will block legitimate work. What is the BEST way to start?",["Enable blocking on all channels on day one to show the business that the tool works","Run the policies in monitor-only mode, tune them, then enable blocking","Skip classification and rely on the tool's default policies alone","Deploy DLP only for executives, who handle the most sensitive data"],1,"Starting in monitor mode shows which rules produce false positives so they can be tuned before enforcement disrupts work. Blocking from day one is the tempting 'secure' choice but often causes business pushback and rule exceptions.","Domain 2: Data loss prevention",["Blocking immediately, before tuning, tends to stop legitimate work and erodes support for DLP.",null,"Default policies without classification miss the company's own sensitive data types.","Sensitive data is handled across the business, so covering only executives leaves large gaps."],2],["x15",0,2,"A company wants its classification of 'Confidential' to travel with documents when they are copied between cloud storage, email and collaboration tools, so each tool can apply the right protections. What should it use?",["Folder names that include the word Confidential","Data labels stored in the documents' metadata","A spreadsheet listing every confidential document","A separate storage account for each classification level"],1,"Labels embedded in metadata move with the file, so DLP, IRM and other tools can read and act on them wherever the file goes. Folder names and separate accounts are lost as soon as a file is copied elsewhere.","Domain 2: Data labeling",["A folder name is lost as soon as the file is copied to another location.",null,"A separate list is quickly out of date and tools cannot read it when handling the file.","Separate storage accounts do not follow a file when it is emailed or shared."],1],["x16",0,2,"A company protects design documents with information rights management and needs to share them with an external engineering partner. What must be in place for the partner to open the files?",["The documents must first be converted to plain text files","The partner must be given the company's IRM master key","The partner must connect to the company's internal network by VPN each time it opens a file","Partner users must have identities the IRM service can authenticate"],3,"IRM checks each user's identity and rights before allowing access, so external users need identities the IRM service trusts, for example through federation. Handing over a master key would defeat the whole purpose of IRM.","Domain 2: Information rights management",["Converting to plain text removes the protection instead of sharing securely.","Sharing a master key breaks IRM and gives the partner unlimited access.","IRM protection travels with the file; network location is not what grants access.",null],2],["x17",0,2,"When writing a data retention policy for cloud-stored invoices, what should FIRST determine how long the invoices are kept?",["Legal, regulatory and contractual requirements","The storage price of the cheapest archive tier","The default retention setting of the provider","How much free space remains in the storage account"],0,"Retention periods come from laws, regulations, contracts and business needs; storage cost and provider defaults are secondary. Keeping data longer than needed also increases risk and discovery exposure.","Domain 2: Data retention",[null,"Storage cost affects where data is kept, not how long it must be kept.","Provider defaults are not based on the company's legal obligations.","Free space is an operational detail and does not set legal retention periods."],1],["x18",0,2,"Investigators need to reconstruct who accessed a sensitive cloud database record. Which set of attributes must the data access logs capture to support accountability?",["Database size, CPU usage and query latency","Encryption algorithm and key length used for storage","User identity, action, object, timestamp and source","Backup schedule and retention period for the table"],2,"Accountability requires knowing who did what, to which data, when and from where. Performance and backup settings are useful operationally but cannot show who accessed a record.","Domain 2: Auditability and accountability",["Performance metrics help capacity planning but do not identify who accessed a record.","Encryption settings describe protection at rest, not access events.",null,"Backup settings describe recovery, not who touched the data."],1],["x19",0,2,"A review finds that a disk snapshot of a production database volume was shared publicly by mistake. Which storage threat does this represent?",["Loss of availability from a failed storage controller","Unauthorized access through a misconfigured storage sharing setting","Data corruption caused by silent bit rot on disk","Performance degradation from a noisy neighbor"],1,"Publicly shared snapshots let anyone copy the full volume, which is a confidentiality breach caused by misconfiguration. Hardware failure and bit rot threaten availability and integrity, not confidentiality.","Domain 2: Threats to storage",["A controller failure affects availability, not who can read the data.",null,"Bit rot affects integrity; it does not expose data to outsiders.","A noisy neighbor affects performance, not confidentiality."],2],["x20",0,2,"A health research team wants to train a model in the cloud on patient records. Reviewers worry the trained model could reveal whether a specific person's record was in the training set. Which control BEST reduces this risk?",["Encrypt the training data at rest with a customer-managed key","Train with differential privacy on de-identified data","Store the model file in a separate cloud account","Increase the number of training epochs"],1,"Differential privacy limits how much any single record influences the model, reducing membership inference and memorization, and de-identification removes direct identifiers. Encryption at rest protects stored data but does nothing once the model has learned from it.","Domain 2: Protecting AI and ML data",["Encryption at rest protects stored files, not what the trained model reveals.",null,"Moving the model file changes where it is stored, not what it can leak.","More epochs tend to increase memorization of individual records, not reduce it."],3],["x21",0,2,"A cloud team stores the data encryption keys for a database in the same storage bucket as the encrypted database backups, 'for convenience'. What is the main problem?",["Keys stored in the same bucket as the backups expire automatically after 90 days","Encrypted backups cannot be compressed","Anyone who obtains the backups also obtains the keys, defeating the encryption","The keys will slow down backup restores"],2,"Keys must be kept separate from the data they protect, ideally in a KMS or HSM with separate access control, so one compromise does not expose both. The other options are not real constraints of storing keys that way.","Domain 2: Key management",["Objects in storage do not expire on their own after 90 days; the issue is exposure, not expiry.","Compression is unrelated to where the keys are stored.",null,"Restore speed is not the concern; storing keys with data removes the protection."],2],["x22",0,2,"A company plans to crypto-shred a customer's data at the end of a contract by deleting the customer's dedicated key. What must be TRUE for this to make the data unrecoverable?",["Every copy, including backups and replicas, was protected only by that key","The storage volumes holding the data were also physically degaussed by the provider's staff","The key was rotated at least once during the contract","The provider has issued a certificate of destruction for the disks"],0,"Crypto-shredding works only if no copy of the data, and no copy of the key, exists outside the key's protection; an unencrypted backup or a key escrow copy would survive. Physical destruction is not required, which is the reason crypto-shredding is used in the cloud.","Domain 2: Crypto-shredding",[null,"Physical degaussing is unnecessary and usually impossible in cloud; that is why crypto-shredding is used.","Rotation does not matter; what matters is that no copy escapes the deleted key.","A destruction certificate for disks does not cover copies stored elsewhere or unencrypted backups."],3],["x23",0,3,"In a multitenant IaaS environment, which single compromise would have the WIDEST impact across customers?",["Compromise of one tenant's guest operating system","A leaked password for one tenant's database","A misconfigured security group on one VM","Compromise of the hypervisor on a shared host"],3,"The hypervisor controls every virtual machine on the host, so compromising it can expose all tenants on that host. The other options affect a single tenant's resources.","Domain 3: Virtualization risks",["One guest OS compromise affects that tenant's VM unless it leads to a hypervisor escape.","A leaked database password affects only that tenant's data.","A security group mistake exposes only the resources it is attached to.",null],2],["x24",0,3,"Which measure MOST directly protects a cloud customer's management plane (console and API access)?",["Full-disk encryption on every virtual machine","A web application firewall in front of all of the customer-facing web applications","Daily backups of all databases","Multifactor authentication and least-privilege roles for administrators"],3,"The management plane controls all resources, so strong authentication and tightly scoped administrative permissions are the key protections. Disk encryption protects data at rest but does not stop someone with stolen console credentials.","Domain 3: Management plane security",["Disk encryption protects data at rest, not access to the console and APIs.","A WAF protects web applications, not the provider's management console and APIs.","Backups help recovery but do not prevent misuse of the management plane.",null],1],["x25",0,3,"During a utility power failure at a data center, what is the role of the uninterruptible power supply (UPS)?",["Bridge the gap until the generators start and stabilize","Provide power for several days without refueling","Replace the need for generators entirely","Protect the building from lightning strikes"],0,"A UPS supplies power for minutes, long enough for generators to start and take the load. Generators, with fuel contracts, supply power for extended outages.","Domain 3: Data center power",[null,"UPS batteries last minutes, not days; generators handle long outages.","Without generators, the data center would go dark once UPS batteries run out.","Lightning protection is handled by grounding and surge protection systems, not the UPS's role."],2],["x26",0,3,"A data center arranges racks so server intakes face each other across one aisle and exhausts face each other across the next, with barriers enclosing one of the aisles. What is the main purpose?",["Make it easier for technicians to reach both sides of a rack","Reduce the number of power circuits needed per rack","Prevent unauthorized people from entering the server hall","Keep cold intake air and hot exhaust air from mixing"],3,"Hot aisle/cold aisle containment separates cold intake air from hot exhaust, so cooling works efficiently and equipment stays within temperature limits. It is an environmental control, not a physical access control.","Domain 3: Environmental design",["Access for technicians is not the goal; containment is about airflow.","Rack layout for airflow does not change how many power circuits are needed.","Aisle containment manages airflow; access to the hall is controlled by other physical measures.",null],2],["x27",0,3,"A provider is choosing a site for a new data center. Which factor matters MOST for physical security and resilience?",["Low exposure to floods, earthquakes and other natural hazards","Proximity to a busy city center for visibility","A building with many public entrances","Being on the same power substation as a nearby airport"],0,"Site selection should minimize natural hazards and provide redundant utilities and good access for staff and emergency services. Visibility and many entrances increase risk instead of reducing it.","Domain 3: Site selection",[null,"Visibility attracts attention; data centers usually keep a low profile.","Many public entrances make physical access harder to control.","Sharing a substation creates a common point of failure rather than redundancy."],1],["x28",0,3,"A company runs a critical application on VMs in a single availability zone of one region. What is the simplest change that protects it against the failure of one data center?",["Buy a larger instance type in the same zone","Take nightly snapshots of every VM and keep them in the same availability zone","Add more CPU monitoring alerts to the existing VMs","Deploy instances across multiple availability zones behind a load balancer"],3,"Availability zones are separate data centers with independent power and networking, so spreading instances across them survives the loss of one. Snapshots stored in the same zone could be lost with it.","Domain 3: Availability design",["A bigger instance is still a single point of failure in one zone.","Snapshots kept in the same zone could be unavailable in the same outage.","More alerts tell you about the failure but do not keep the application running.",null],2],["x29",0,3,"A DR plan describes in detail how to fail over to a secondary cloud region but says nothing more. After a real failover, the team struggles to move back. What was missing from the plan?",["A list of the vendor's sales contacts","Failback procedures to return operations to the primary site","A description of the company's org chart","A copy of the provider's marketing materials"],1,"A complete DR plan covers returning to normal operations, including data resynchronization and switching traffic back, which can be as risky as the failover itself. The other options add no recovery capability.","Domain 3: BC/DR plans",["Sales contacts do not help restore operations to the primary site.",null,"An org chart does not describe the technical steps needed to return to normal.","Marketing materials have no place in a recovery plan."],3],["x30",0,3,"Investigators are correlating events from cloud audit logs, VM system logs and a SaaS application, but the order of events does not make sense. What is the MOST likely cause?",["Clocks were not synchronized to a common time source","The logs were stored in compressed format","The logs were encrypted at rest","The SIEM received the logs over TLS"],0,"Correlation depends on accurate, synchronized timestamps, ideally in UTC from a reliable time source such as NTP. Compression, encryption at rest and TLS protect or store logs but do not change the recorded times.","Domain 3: Audit mechanisms",[null,"Compression changes storage size, not the timestamps inside the records.","Encryption at rest protects stored logs without changing their content.","TLS protects logs in transit and does not alter event times."],2],["x31",0,3,"A company processes cryptographic keys on cloud VMs and is concerned about side-channel attacks from other customers' VMs on the same physical host. Which option BEST reduces this risk?",["Place the VMs in a different subnet","Use a larger VM size on shared hosts","Run the workload on dedicated, single-tenant hosts","Enable a host-based firewall on each VM"],2,"Dedicated hosts ensure no other customer's VM shares the physical hardware, removing the co-residency that cross-tenant side channels depend on. Network controls like subnets and firewalls do not affect shared CPU caches or memory.","Domain 3: Multitenancy risks",["Subnets separate network traffic, not the physical CPU and memory that side channels exploit.","A larger VM on a shared host may still share hardware with other tenants.",null,"Firewalls filter network traffic and do nothing about hardware side channels."],3],["x32",0,3,"A network IDS appliance monitors traffic at the physical edge of a private cloud, yet attacks between two VMs on the same host go unseen. Why, and what helps?",["The IDS needs a faster uplink; upgrade it to 100 Gbps","Same-host VM traffic stays on the virtual switch; use virtual or host-based monitoring","The hypervisor encrypts all traffic between VMs on a host; disable that encryption so the IDS can read it","The IDS signatures are old; update them weekly"],1,"East-west traffic between VMs on the same host never leaves the virtual switch, so a physical sensor cannot see it; virtual taps, virtual IDS or host agents are needed. Updating signatures does not help if the traffic never reaches the sensor.","Domain 3: Virtual network visibility",["A faster uplink does not help when the traffic never leaves the host.",null,"Hypervisors do not encrypt local VM traffic by default, and disabling encryption would reduce security.","Signatures only matter if the sensor can see the traffic in the first place."],3],["x33",0,4,"A manager denies approving a large cloud purchase, and the application has no record showing who clicked Approve. Which STRIDE threat was not mitigated?",["Repudiation","Tampering","Information disclosure","Denial of service"],0,"Repudiation is the ability to deny performing an action because there is no trustworthy evidence, which is mitigated by secure audit logs and signatures. Tampering is unauthorized modification of data, not the absence of proof.","Domain 4: STRIDE",[null,"Tampering is changing data without authorization; here the problem is missing proof of who acted.","Information disclosure is exposing data to unauthorized people, which did not happen here.","Denial of service makes a system unavailable; the application worked but kept no evidence."],1],["x34",0,4,"A team has listed 40 threats for a cloud application and needs to rank them by damage potential, reproducibility, exploitability, affected users and discoverability. Which model fits?",["STRIDE","PASTA","ATASM","DREAD"],3,"DREAD scores threats on those five factors to prioritize them. STRIDE is used to identify and categorize threats, not rank them.","Domain 4: Threat modeling",["STRIDE categorizes threats by type but does not score them for priority.","PASTA is a seven-stage risk-centric process, not a five-factor scoring model.","ATASM stands for architecture, threats, attack surfaces and mitigations, a way to structure analysis, not scoring.",null],2],["x35",0,4,"A user of a cloud invoicing app changes the invoice number in the URL from 1041 to 1042 and sees another customer's invoice. Which OWASP Top 10 category does this fall under?",["Broken access control","Cryptographic failures","Injection","Security logging and monitoring failures"],0,"The application does not check that the user is authorized to see the requested object, an insecure direct object reference, which is part of broken access control. No code or query was injected, so it is not injection.","Domain 4: OWASP Top 10",[null,"Cryptographic failures concern weak or missing encryption, not missing authorization checks.","Nothing was injected into a query or command; the user simply requested another record.","Poor logging makes attacks harder to detect but is not the flaw that exposed the invoice."],1],["x36",0,4,"A team wants security testing that runs inside the application during functional QA tests, watching code execution and data flow to report exactly which line caused a flaw. Which approach is this?",["Interactive application security testing (IAST)","Static application security testing (SAST)","Dynamic application security testing (DAST)","Software composition analysis (SCA)"],0,"IAST places an agent inside the running application and observes it while tests exercise it, combining runtime context with code-level detail. DAST tests the running app from the outside and cannot point to the exact line of code.","Domain 4: Application security testing",[null,"SAST analyzes code without running it, so it does not observe runtime data flow.","DAST probes from the outside and cannot see which line of code is responsible.","SCA inventories third-party components and their known vulnerabilities, not custom code behavior."],3],["x37",0,4,"A cloud customer asks a SaaS vendor to prove which open-source components and versions are in its product, so the customer can respond quickly to new vulnerabilities. What should the vendor provide?",["A penetration test executive summary","A software bill of materials (SBOM)","The vendor's business continuity plan","A list of the vendor's employees with admin access"],1,"An SBOM lists the components and versions in a product, letting customers check whether a newly disclosed vulnerability affects them. A penetration test summary shows tested weaknesses but not a component inventory.","Domain 4: Software supply chain",["A pentest summary reports findings at one point in time, not the full component inventory.",null,"A continuity plan covers recovery, not which libraries the product contains.","An admin list is about access control, not software components."],2],["x38",0,4,"Besides testing that a password reset feature works, testers write scenarios in which an attacker requests thousands of resets for one account or guesses reset tokens. What are these scenarios called?",["Use cases","Regression tests","Load tests","Abuse cases (misuse cases)"],3,"Abuse cases describe how an attacker might misuse a feature, so the team can test the defenses. Use cases describe intended, legitimate behavior, which is what the functional tests already cover.","Domain 4: Software assurance",["Use cases describe legitimate user goals, not how attackers would misuse the feature.","Regression tests confirm old features still work after changes.","Load tests measure performance under volume, not intentional abuse by an attacker.",null],2],["x39",0,4,"A legacy cloud service exchanges SOAP messages with partners. The security team wants a component in front of it that validates messages against the schema, blocks malicious XML such as external entity references and handles WS-Security signatures. What fits BEST?",["A database activity monitor","A network load balancer","An XML gateway","A cloud access security broker"],2,"An XML gateway (XML firewall) inspects and validates XML traffic, enforces schemas, rejects dangerous constructs and can process XML signatures and encryption. A network load balancer distributes traffic without inspecting message content.","Domain 4: Application architecture",["A database activity monitor watches queries to databases, not XML messages from partners.","A network load balancer spreads traffic but does not inspect XML content.",null,"A CASB controls use of cloud services by users, not partner SOAP traffic to an app."],3],["x40",0,4,"A company is choosing a federation protocol for a new cloud-native mobile app that needs to sign users in and receive a compact, JSON-based identity token. Which protocol fits BEST?",["OpenID Connect","SAML 2.0","LDAP","Kerberos"],0,"OpenID Connect adds an authentication layer on OAuth 2.0 and returns a JSON Web Token as the ID token, which suits mobile and modern web apps. SAML 2.0 uses XML assertions and browser redirects, which fit enterprise web SSO better than native mobile apps.","Domain 4: Federated identity",[null,"SAML uses XML assertions and is aimed mainly at browser-based enterprise SSO.","LDAP is a directory protocol, not an internet federation protocol for mobile apps.","Kerberos is designed for internal networks with a key distribution center, not internet mobile apps."],2],["x41",0,4,"A CI/CD pipeline deploys to a cloud account using a long-lived access key stored as a pipeline variable. What is the MOST secure replacement?",["Keep the stored access key but rotate it every 90 days and restrict who can view pipeline variables","Encode the access key in Base64 before storing it","Use workload identity federation to get short-lived credentials for each run","Store the access key in the source code repository"],2,"Workload identity federation lets the pipeline exchange a signed token for temporary cloud credentials, so no long-lived secret exists to leak. Rotating the key every 90 days is better than nothing but still leaves a valid static secret exposed for months.","Domain 4: Secrets management",["Rotation shortens exposure but still leaves a usable long-lived secret in the pipeline.","Base64 is an encoding, not encryption, so the key is still readable.",null,"Keys in source control are one of the most common causes of credential leaks."],3],["x42",0,4,"After a supply chain attack in which a build server was compromised, a company wants its cloud deployment system to accept only container images produced by its trusted pipeline and not altered since. What should it require?",["Images pulled only from the most popular public registry","Signed images whose signatures and provenance are verified at deploy time","Images scanned once by a vulnerability scanner last quarter","Images whose tag is named 'latest'"],1,"Signing images in the trusted pipeline and verifying signatures and build provenance before deployment ensures integrity and origin. A past vulnerability scan does not prove the image came from the pipeline or was not changed afterward.","Domain 4: Verified secure software",["Popularity of a registry says nothing about who built the image or whether it was altered.",null,"An old scan finds known vulnerabilities but does not prove origin or integrity.","A tag name is mutable and can point to any image."],3],["x43",0,5,"A payment processor running in the cloud must generate and store encryption keys in tamper-resistant hardware validated to FIPS 140-3, and perform high-volume cryptographic operations. What should it use?",["A trusted platform module (TPM) in each VM","A hardware security module (HSM)","A software keystore on the application server","A password manager for the operations team"],1,"An HSM is dedicated, tamper-resistant hardware for generating, storing and using keys at scale, and cloud HSM services offer FIPS 140-3 validated modules. A TPM is a chip for platform integrity and a small set of keys on one machine, not a shared high-volume key service.","Domain 5: Hardware security",["A TPM protects one machine's boot integrity and keys; it is not built for high-volume shared key operations.",null,"A software keystore lacks hardware tamper resistance and validated protection.","A password manager stores human credentials, not cryptographic keys for payment processing."],1],["x44",0,5,"A platform team no longer logs in to production servers to apply patches. Instead, it builds a new patched image and replaces the running instances with it. What is this approach called?",["Immutable infrastructure","Configuration drift","In-place upgrade","Snowflake servers"],0,"With immutable infrastructure, servers are never modified after deployment; changes are made by building and deploying new images, which keeps environments consistent. In-place upgrades change running servers, the practice being replaced here.","Domain 5: Hardening and patching",[null,"Configuration drift is the problem of servers diverging from their intended state, not a deployment approach.","In-place upgrades modify running servers, which is exactly what the team stopped doing.","Snowflake servers are unique, hand-configured machines, the opposite of this approach."],2],["x45",0,5,"Infrastructure is defined in Terraform. The security team wants to catch storage buckets without encryption and security groups open to the internet before anything is deployed. What should it add?",["A monthly manual review of the production console","Packet capture on the production network","Policy-as-code scanning of the IaC templates in the CI pipeline","An annual penetration test of the application"],2,"Scanning IaC templates with policy-as-code rules in CI stops misconfigurations before they reach any environment. Monthly console reviews only find problems after they are already live.","Domain 5: Infrastructure as code",["Monthly reviews find issues only after misconfigured resources are live.","Packet capture shows traffic, not whether a template's settings are compliant.",null,"An annual pentest is too late and too infrequent to stop each misconfiguration."],2],["x46",0,5,"A critical vulnerability is being actively exploited, and a firewall rule change must be made in production tonight, outside the normal change advisory board schedule. What should happen under a mature change management process?",["Make the change without any record, because it is urgent","Wait for the next scheduled CAB meeting in two weeks","Use the emergency change process, with approval by the emergency authority and review afterward","Ask the cloud provider's support team to make the firewall change on the customer's behalf tonight"],2,"Emergency changes follow an expedited path with a designated approver, are documented and are reviewed after implementation. Skipping records entirely breaks traceability; waiting two weeks leaves the system exposed.","Domain 5: Change management",["Unrecorded changes break traceability and can cause configuration drift and outages.","Waiting two weeks leaves an actively exploited vulnerability open.",null,"The provider does not manage customer firewall rules in IaaS; the customer owns this change."],3],["x47",0,5,"Under ITIL, what distinguishes release management from deployment management?",["They are two names for the same process","Release makes features available to users; deployment moves components into environments","Release management approves changes; deployment management records and tracks incidents after go-live","Deployment management plans capacity; release management handles problems"],1,"In ITIL 4, deployment management moves new or changed components to live or other environments, while release management makes new and changed services and features available for use, which can happen separately, for example with feature flags. Change approval belongs to change enablement.","Domain 5: ITIL practices",["ITIL treats them as separate practices, since deploying and releasing can happen at different times.",null,"Change approval is change enablement, and incidents belong to incident management.","Capacity planning and problem handling are separate practices from both."],3],["x48",0,5,"A responder copies a disk image of a compromised cloud VM for investigation. What should she do immediately after acquiring it to show later that the evidence was not altered?",["Compress the image to save storage space","Rename the image with a shorter file name","Mount the image read-write to look through it quickly","Calculate and record a cryptographic hash of the image"],3,"Hashing the image at acquisition and recording it in the chain of custody lets anyone verify later that the evidence is unchanged. Mounting it read-write could modify the evidence and undermine it in court.","Domain 5: Digital forensics",["Compression does not prove integrity and is not a substitute for a recorded hash.","Renaming changes nothing about proving the content is unaltered.","Read-write mounting can change the evidence; analysis should use read-only copies.",null],1],["x49",0,5,"A vulnerability scan of cloud VMs from outside the hosts reports few issues, but the team suspects outdated packages inside the operating systems. What change would give a more complete picture?",["Run authenticated (credentialed) scans or agent-based scanning","Scan more often from the same external position","Scan only the load balancer's public address","Turn off the host firewalls during scans permanently"],0,"Authenticated or agent-based scans can inspect installed packages and configuration directly, finding vulnerabilities an external scan cannot see. Scanning more often from outside repeats the same limited view.","Domain 5: Vulnerability assessment",[null,"More frequent unauthenticated scans still cannot see inside the operating system.","Scanning only the load balancer sees even less of the environment.","Permanently disabling firewalls weakens security and still does not show installed package versions."],2],["x50",0,5,"A cloud customer confirms that personal data of EU residents was exposed. Who should coordinate the notification to regulators and affected people?",["Whichever engineer first discovered the exposure","The cloud provider's public relations team","No one should notify anybody until the cloud provider completes its own investigation of the exposure","The incident response lead, with legal and privacy, per the communication plan"],3,"Notifications have legal deadlines and content requirements, so they are coordinated by designated roles with legal and privacy input under the incident communication plan. Waiting for the provider can miss deadlines that apply to the customer as controller.","Domain 5: Communicating with stakeholders",["Individual engineers should escalate, not notify regulators on their own.","The provider does not speak for the customer, which is usually the controller with the duty to notify.","Waiting can miss legal notification deadlines that apply to the customer.",null],2],["x51",0,5,"A SOC uses a large language model to summarize alerts and suggest response steps. What is the MOST important control for using its output?",["Let the model close alerts automatically to save analyst time","Send the model all raw logs, including secrets and credentials, so it has the fullest context","Analysts validate the summaries and approve any response action before it runs","Use the model's confidence wording as proof of accuracy"],2,"LLMs can produce confident but wrong statements, so human review of conclusions and actions is essential, especially for containment steps. Sharing secrets with the model creates a new data exposure risk.","Domain 5: AI in security operations",["Automatic closure lets model mistakes hide real incidents.","Sending secrets to the model exposes sensitive data and does not improve accuracy safely.",null,"Confident language from a model is not evidence that its conclusions are correct."],3],["x52",0,5,"A team backs up a busy database VM with crash-consistent disk snapshots. A test restore shows the database needs lengthy repair and some recent transactions are missing. What should it use instead?",["More frequent crash-consistent snapshots, taken every 15 minutes, of only the operating system disk","Application-consistent backups that quiesce or use the database's own backup tools","Copies of the database files taken while the service is writing","Snapshots stored in a cheaper archive tier"],1,"Application-consistent backups flush and pause writes, or use native database backup methods, so the restored database is in a clean, transactionally consistent state. Copying live files while writing makes inconsistency worse.","Domain 5: Backup and restore",["Snapshotting only the OS disk leaves out the database data entirely.",null,"Copying files mid-write produces inconsistent, possibly corrupt backups.","The storage tier affects cost, not whether the backup is consistent."],3],["x53",0,6,"Under the GDPR, a controller becomes aware of a personal data breach that is likely to result in a risk to people's rights. Within what time should it normally notify the supervisory authority?",["24 hours","30 days","72 hours","90 days"],2,"GDPR Article 33 requires notification to the supervisory authority without undue delay and, where feasible, within 72 hours of becoming aware. Thirty days is a common deadline in other contexts but not the GDPR regulator notice.","Domain 6: Privacy breach notification",["Twenty-four hours is not the GDPR requirement for notifying the supervisory authority.","Thirty days is far longer than the GDPR allows for notifying the authority.",null,"Ninety days would badly miss the GDPR notification deadline."],1],["x54",0,6,"A company receives notice of a lawsuit and must preserve all related email and files stored in its SaaS collaboration platform. What should it do FIRST?",["Export everything to the legal team's personal laptops for safekeeping until trial","Apply a legal hold in the platform to the relevant custodians and content","Delete unrelated content to make the search easier","Wait until the court requests the data before acting"],1,"Once litigation is reasonably anticipated, the duty to preserve applies, and a legal hold stops relevant data from being deleted or altered, including by retention policies. Deleting content during a hold could be seen as spoliation of evidence.","Domain 6: eDiscovery",["Copying data to personal devices breaks custody and creates new security risks.",null,"Deleting content after notice of litigation can be treated as destroying evidence.","The duty to preserve starts when litigation is anticipated, not when the court asks."],2],["x55",0,6,"A company's external financial auditor wants assurance about a cloud payroll provider's controls that affect the company's financial statements. Which report is designed for this?",["SOC 2","SOC 1","SOC 3","ISO/IEC 27001 certificate"],1,"SOC 1 reports cover controls at a service organization relevant to user entities' internal control over financial reporting. SOC 2 addresses the trust services criteria such as security and availability, not financial reporting controls.","Domain 6: Audit reports",["SOC 2 covers security, availability and related criteria, not controls over financial reporting.",null,"SOC 3 is a general-use summary without the detail an auditor needs.","An ISO/IEC 27001 certificate covers the security management system, not financial reporting controls."],3],["x56",0,6,"A SaaS provider's SOC 2 report states that its data center hosting is performed by a subservice organization using the carve-out method. What does this mean for the customer?",["The hosting provider's controls were fully described, tested and included in the auditor's opinion","The customer no longer needs any assurance about hosting","Hosting controls are excluded; the customer should get assurance on them separately","The SaaS provider is not allowed to use subservice organizations"],2,"Under the carve-out method, the subservice organization's controls are excluded from the description and testing, so the customer should review that organization's own report. With the inclusive method, those controls would be included and tested.","Domain 6: Audit methodologies",["That describes the inclusive method; carve-out excludes the subservice controls.","Hosting controls still matter; they are simply not covered by this report.",null,"Using subservice organizations is common and allowed; the report just discloses it."],3],["x57",0,6,"The board states that the company will accept moderate risk in pursuing new cloud products but no risk that could lead to regulatory sanctions. What has the board defined?",["Risk appetite","Risk transfer","Residual risk","Inherent risk"],0,"Risk appetite is the amount and type of risk an organization is willing to pursue or accept to meet its objectives. Residual risk is what remains after controls, not a statement of willingness.","Domain 6: Enterprise risk management",[null,"Risk transfer shifts impact to another party, such as through insurance or contracts.","Residual risk is the risk left after controls, not what the board is willing to accept.","Inherent risk is the level of risk before any controls are applied."],2],["x58",0,6,"A customer's contract template includes a right to perform on-site audits of the provider's data centers. A large public cloud provider rejects this clause. What is the provider MOST likely to offer instead?",["Independent third-party audit reports and certifications","Remote administrator access to its hypervisors","A copy of its internal network diagrams for all regions","A guarantee that no security incidents will occur"],0,"Hyperscale providers rarely allow individual on-site audits because of multitenancy and security, and instead share independent reports such as SOC 2 and ISO/IEC 27001 certificates. Giving customers hypervisor access would itself create a major security risk.","Domain 6: Cloud contracts",[null,"Customer access to hypervisors would compromise the isolation of all tenants.","Detailed internal network diagrams are sensitive and are not shared with individual customers.","No provider can guarantee that incidents will never occur."],3],["x59",0,6,"A company plans to use a cloud-hosted AI system to screen job applicants for roles in the EU. How is this use MOST likely classified under the EU AI Act?",["Minimal risk, with no specific obligations","High-risk, with obligations such as risk management, data governance, human oversight and logging","Prohibited in all circumstances","Exempt, because the AI system is hosted and operated by a third-party cloud provider rather than the company"],1,"The EU AI Act lists AI systems used in employment, such as recruiting and screening candidates, as high-risk, which brings requirements for risk management, data quality, transparency, human oversight and record-keeping. Recruitment screening is not among the banned practices.","Domain 6: AI regulation",["Employment and recruitment uses are specifically listed as high-risk, not minimal risk.",null,"Recruitment screening is regulated as high-risk, not banned outright.","Where the system is hosted does not remove obligations from providers and deployers."],3],["x60",0,6,"An electric utility in North America wants to move systems that monitor its bulk power grid to a cloud service. Which compliance requirements are MOST specific to this scenario?",["PCI DSS","HIPAA Security Rule","GLBA Safeguards Rule","NERC CIP standards"],3,"NERC CIP (Critical Infrastructure Protection) standards apply to the bulk electric system in North America and govern the protection of its cyber assets. PCI DSS applies to payment card data, which is not the concern here.","Domain 6: Specialized compliance",["PCI DSS protects payment card data, not grid control systems.","HIPAA covers health information, not power grid systems.","GLBA applies to financial institutions' customer information.",null],2]]);
